# AGENTS.md — how to use the {{product}} documentation

This repository (or workspace) contains {{product}} ({{code_name}}) and its complete documentation in `{{docs_dir}}/`. Read this before explaining or changing any part of the system. It applies to every coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Copilot, Gemini …).

## 0. Locate things first

- **Docs root** = the folder containing `{{docs_dir}}/agent/entities.jsonl` (repository root, `repo-kit/` when a shared package was unpacked as is, or search for it).
- **Source root** = `{{source_root}}` relative to the docs root, or the repository root. If there is no code, answer from the docs with citations and say the answers are not verified against the code.
- Run `lookup.py` **from the workspace root** by its path (`python <docs root>/{{docs_dir}}/_tools/lookup.py <Name>`); never `cd` into the docs first. If `python` is not found (on Windows it may open the Microsoft Store), use `py -3` or `python3`.

## 1. What exists

| Resource | Path | Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Page map | `{{docs_dir}}/llms.txt` | One line per page with its purpose: read first |
| Entity index | `{{docs_dir}}/agent/entities.jsonl` | Every documented item: `kind`, `name`, `file`, `anchor`, `summary` |
| Lookup | `python {{docs_dir}}/_tools/lookup.py <Name> [--kind K] [--list] [--fuzzy] [--find TEXT] [--src PATH] [--no-code]` | Prints `doc: path:line` and `src: path:line` per match, then the entry, then the code (the declaration, or each cited source line); an exact name shows only itself (`--fuzzy` adds partial matches); finding ids (`SEC-02`) and narrative table rows are indexed too; `--find` lists matching source lines |
| Search by topic | `python {{docs_dir}}/_tools/lookup.py --search "<question in words>"` | Ranked docs sections and code items with `doc:` / `src:` links, when the topic is known but not the name |
| Call trees | `python {{docs_dir}}/_tools/trace_calls.py <Class.Method> [--up] [--entry]` | Callees; callers (`--up`, impact of a change); endpoints, screens and jobs that reach it (`--entry`) |
| Narrative docs | `{{docs_dir}}/<section>/` | Behaviour, rules, flows, integrations, security |
| Reference | `{{docs_dir}}/reference/` | Exhaustive generated catalogues with anchors |
| Research notes | `{{docs_dir}}/_notes/` | Dense working notes per area, written before the pages: where a note and a page disagree, the page wins (check the code) |
| Code graph | `graphify-out/` (if built) | `graphify query / explain / path / affected` |

Kinds: {{kinds}}.

## 2. Lookup order

1. `{{docs_dir}}/llms.txt` → pick pages; a topic without a name → `lookup.py --search "<words>"`.
2. `lookup.py <Name>` for each named item (entry, code, connections, findings that name it).
3. Narrative page for behaviour.
4. Reference entry (`doc:` line) for exact details; `src:` line for the code; `--find` for the exact line to change.
5. `trace_calls.py` and graphify for relationships and impact. For a call through an interface, base class, message, event or delegate, find what actually runs: the method map labels it (`(di registration)`, `(override)`, `(message)`, `(event)` …) and `{{docs_dir}}/reference/dependency-injection.md` (C#, when present) lists registrations per host, lifetimes, decorators, handlers and subscribers. {{db_graph_note}}
6. Verify in source before editing.

